<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RUT_SheenProtection.xml                RotSporeKit - ROT_SHEEN_WEATHER_1 build pass

  Apparel-summed protection stat for RUT_SheenCoating's exposure clock (owner
  card 5, RULED 2026-09-17: "gear only slows the exposure clock, never full
  immunity"). Shape cribbed directly from
  src/RimMandrake/EnvironmentalHazards/Defs/StatDefs/RM_WetBulbProtection.xml
  (ParentName="ArmorRatingBase", maxValue overridden to 1, no StatPart_Stuff
  since no vendored Stuff in this repo grants it - apparel carries it via a
  flat statBases entry, same reasoning that file gives).

  Summed across worn apparel by HazardTargeting.SumApparelStat
  (RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards) rather than read as a single pawn stat -
  an "Apparel"-category StatDef has no vanilla per-pawn aggregation
  (ArmorUtility is the only vanilla reader, and it reads per-apparel-item).

  The ported chitin spider helmet (RUT_Apparel_ChitinSpiderHelmet,
  ThingDefs_Apparel/RUT_RotSporeKit_ChitinSpiderHelmet.xml) is the only
  carrier this pass, at 0.75 - exactly the ruled 4x slowdown
  (1 - 0.75 = 0.25 = 1/4 rate). RM_HediffComp_SheenExposure's own
  minDriveFactor (0.05) is the structural backstop against a future item
  stacking this toward 1.0 and reaching full immunity, which card 5 rules
  out permanently.
-->
<Defs>

  <StatDef ParentName="ArmorRatingBase">
    <defName>RUT_SheenProtection</defName>
    <label>Sheen protection</label>
    <labelForFullStatList>Sheen protection (armor stat)</labelForFullStatList>
    <description>How much this garment slows the Sheen's exposure clock — sealed or oiled gear built to shed the Rot's own glossy sleet, not ordinary insulation. Ordinary rain gear does nothing against it; only apparel carrying this stat helps.

Summed across every garment worn at once, then capped. It only ever slows the clock — no amount of gear stops it outright; only the Rot's own symbiosis does that.</description>
    <maxValue>1</maxValue>
    <parts>
      <li Class="StatPart_Quality">
        <factorAwful>0.6</factorAwful>
        <factorPoor>0.8</factorPoor>
        <factorNormal>1</factorNormal>
        <factorGood>1.15</factorGood>
        <factorExcellent>1.3</factorExcellent>
        <factorMasterwork>1.45</factorMasterwork>
        <factorLegendary>1.8</factorLegendary>
      </li>
    </parts>
  </StatDef>

</Defs>
